syntax:
template <class Arg, class Result>
std::pointer_to_unary_function<Arg, Result> std::ptr_fun(Result (*f)(Arg));
// Implementation notes: This template function returns an object of type std::pointer_to_unary_function<Arg, Result>, which is a struct derived from std::unary_function<Arg, Result>. The struct contains a private member: Result (*ptr_)(Arg); and a public operator(): Result operator()(Arg arg) const { return ptr_(arg); }. The compiler instantiates this template based on the types of Arg and Result, generating a new class for each unique combination, which can lead to code bloat if used extensively.

code_example:
#include <functional>
#include <iostream>

int add_one(int x) {
    return x + 1;  // Simple unary function
}

int main() {
    auto func_obj = std::ptr_fun(add_one);  // Adapts function pointer to functor
    std::cout << func_obj(5) << std::endl;  // Invokes the functor
    return 0;
}
